# Bracha 1987, "Asynchronous Byzantine Agreement Protocols"
# Information and Computation 75, 130-143 (1987)
#
# Figure 1, BPR's replies and its answer: what a (ready, v) arriving
# without the RECEIVED annotation does, what this process's accept does
# for each (ready, v) held from before it, and what p's (initial, v) or
# BESIDE arriving at a process that holds both does.  Three tables, in
# their own file because their inputs read nothing of the run of
# bracha87Fig1.dtc but whether it took step 3: compiled with them, dtc
# carries them down every path of the figure's dispatch for three
# leaves that read them.  Compiled alone it is a small dispatch, run
# once per event after the main dispatch, as bracha87Fig3.dtc runs
# after Figure 1's.
#
# The annotations are the caller's, riding the (ready, v) BPR already
# re-sends (bracha87Fig1.dtc, BPR's rows): ACCEPTED, the sender has
# accepted, and RECEIVED, set per recipient, the sender holds that
# recipient's accept, a (ready, v) from it carrying ACCEPTED.  A
# (ready, v) carrying ACCEPTED is recorded as its sender having
# accepted by the C, beside the ready itself, before the main dispatch;
# that record retires the re-send toward the sender.  A (ready, v)
# arriving without RECEIVED is its sender's word that it does not hold
# this process's accept.  Once this process has an accept to carry, it
# replies to that arrival: its (ready, v), re-sent to the sender,
# carrying ACCEPTED, and RECEIVED where the sender's own accept is
# recorded here.  A (ready, v) carrying RECEIVED draws no reply, so an
# exchange ends in two: the reply to an unmarked arrival carries
# ACCEPTED, so the sender's reply to that, made only where it lacked
# RECEIVED and the sender has accepted, carries RECEIVED.  A lost reply,
# or a lost answer (the second table), is replied to again on its
# target's next unmarked re-send, which is the loss recovery.  The
# first table runs on a duplicate (ready, v), which an unmarked re-send
# is, so it sees it.
#
# The reply is one message, to the sender of the arrival that asked
# for it, on that arrival: no record outlives the event, and nothing
# waits for this process's next sweep.  A forged unmarked (ready, v)
# costs this process one marked (ready, v) per forged arrival, sent
# back to the forger: one message out for one in, at the forger's
# rate, and no record of what is owed a correct process changes.  A
# caller that bounds its egress chooses the order in which it serves
# replies, answers and re-sends.
#
# Inputs:
#   "the arrival is (ready, v)"      { yes, no }
#                                    the event is the arrival of a
#                                    (ready, v); RECEIVED is read only
#                                    then
#   "carries RECEIVED"               { yes, no }
#                                    the (ready, v) carries the sender's
#                                    RECEIVED
#   "had accepted"                   { yes, no }
#                                    Figure 1's "have accepted" at the
#                                    event's start: the arrival that
#                                    fires the accept draws no reply;
#                                    the second table answers its
#                                    sender with every sender of a
#                                    ready held, or, where step 2 is
#                                    taken on the same event, the send
#                                    to all reaches it
#
# Output:
#   "re-send (ready, v) to the sender"  { yes, no }

@"re-send (ready, v) to the sender","the arrival is (ready, v)","carries RECEIVED","had accepted"
yes,yes,no,yes
no,yes,no,no
no,yes,yes,
no,no,,

# The answer at the accept.  A (ready, v) that arrived before this
# process accepted was its sender's word as much as one after: no
# correct process's ready can carry RECEIVED before this process has an
# accept for it to hold.  Before the accept there was nothing to carry,
# and the figure's step 3 sends nothing, so the word is answered when
# the answer exists: on the event that takes step 3, this process's
# (ready, v), re-sent to each process whose (ready, v) it holds, self
# among them, carrying ACCEPTED, and RECEIVED where that process's
# accept is recorded here.  The (ready, v) held is the record of who
# asked, kept by the figure already; nothing new is recorded.  Without
# it, a sender whose ready came early is replied to only after its own
# next re-send, on a sweep: a re-send of a ready that was never lost.
# Where step 2 is taken on the same event, its send to all carries
# ACCEPTED already, and there is nothing to answer.  Each held sender
# is answered once, at the accept, and after that replied to by the
# first table: one message out for each ready in, as there.
#
# Inputs:
#   "accepted on this event"         { yes, no }  step 3 taken on it
#   "had sent ready"                 { yes, no }  Figure 1's "have sent
#                                    ready" at the event's start
#
# Output:
#   "re-send (ready, v) to the processes whose (ready, v) is held"  { yes, no }

@"re-send (ready, v) to the processes whose (ready, v) is held","accepted on this event","had sent ready"
yes,yes,yes
no,yes,no
no,no,

# The echo's reply.  HOLDING rides the echo and the ready, and both
# retire: the echo at this process's accept, the ready once the
# ACCEPTED exchange is done.  A process that comes to hold p's
# (initial, v) and BESIDE only after that has nothing left to carry
# its word, and p re-sends to it until abandon.  So p's (initial, v)
# or BESIDE arriving at a process that holds both and had echoed
# before it came is p's word that it has not heard, and is replied to:
# this process's (echo, v), re-sent to p, carrying HOLDING.  The
# arrival that completes the holding, or fires the echo, is not that
# word: the echo it fires carries HOLDING itself.  The same shape as
# the ready's reply: the message that asks is the one that was sent.
#
# Inputs:
#   "the arrival is (initial, v) or BESIDE"  { yes, no }  from p, as the
#                                    C has already checked
#   "holds both"                     { yes, no }  (initial, v) and
#                                    BESIDE from p, after this event
#   "had echoed"                     { yes, no }  Figure 1's "have
#                                    echoed" at the event's start
#
# Output:
#   "re-send (echo, v) to p"         { yes, no }

@"re-send (echo, v) to p","the arrival is (initial, v) or BESIDE","holds both","had echoed"
yes,yes,yes,yes
no,yes,yes,no
no,yes,no,
no,no,,
